A||N||4||lews84||84 brake hose & caliper replacement||||5
Z||000000||lews84||11-21-1999||06:51 PM||||I took my 84 in because the brakes were mushy and both right side wheels were hot after a short drive. I assumed seized calipers. The garage told me I needed all new calipers, hoses and rotors (because they were pitted) for $1500. Ouch!<P>I decided to do the job myself and ordered the calipers, hoses and carbo-metalic pads from the Fiero store. I started on the right front. I finally got the rivet off by drilling it out and then using the chisel. Typing this is hard because I hit my left index finger instead of the chisel (ARG!). Now I am trying to unhook the hose from the steel line and it won't budge. The U clip is off and I hit the treads with WD40.<P>Any suggestions on how to get the hose unscrewed? Also the steel line 'nut' seems to be smaller than 1/2" and bigger than 7/16", what size is it?<P>I also haven't been able to budge the torx bolts holding the caliper. Again any suggestions? I may be getting to old for this stuff.<P>This car has been in NY & CT all its life so it has substantial rust.<P>The rotors look like they only have tiny pits. Can I use them as is or do I need to replace them also?<P>Thanks in advace.<BR>Lew||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||11-21-1999||07:39 PM||||It's probably a 12 mm size on the hose. Line wrenches work the best or a large pair of vice grips once you've rounded the shoulders with a regular open end wrench. To remove the caliper bolts, you probably just need more leverage. The rotors can probably be turned.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-21-1999||08:57 PM||||While pulling my engine, I disconnected the calipers and I can say the steel lines running into the rubber hoses were STUCK.  WD-40 wouldn't do it. But, if you tap the side of the connection where the hanger is with a hammer while pulling on the 12mm wrench it popped loose real quick, both times. Helps if you have two people, but is doable with one. Hope this helps.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||lews84||11-21-1999||09:16 PM||||Thanks Batboy & Fiero56! I'll dig out my metrics and small hammer tomorrow and take another crack at it. I'll also be looking for a cheater pipe for the 3/8" rachet for the torx tool. I wonder if Sears has a 3/8" breaker bar or maybe a 1/2" to 3/8" adaptor for my 1/2" tools.<P>Why do I continue to be suprised when half the stuff in metric and the other half is English.<P>Hay! I just noticed that I have been promoted to member!<p>[This message has been edited by lews84 (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||theogre||11-21-1999||09:19 PM||||Get yourself a set of "flair nut" wrenches. They will grab better on the nuts to prevent stripping. Sears has these in a set for like 25-30$us. You'll want them for other things like fuel lines. You can put allot more force on them without stripping the nut or slipping off.<P>Reminder/Warning: your new rotors must be cleaned with brake cleaner or alcohol to remove all oil before you put the new calipers and pads on.<P>You can apply anti sieze to the lug stud threads to make the wheels easier to work on with hand tools. DO NOT allow anti sieze to get on the cone faces of the lug nuts or the lug holes in the wheels. You want the cones to bind. That's what keeps them from comming loose. DO NOT allow air tools to tighten anti siezed lugs. You will over tighten them if you do.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-21-1999).]||||reg||1
